WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Data Protection uses a key ring to protect and unprotect payloads.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.120
The key ring contains key material and metadata used by the framework.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:17.500
New Protect operations use an active default key, while Unprotect may need older keys to read payloads that were created before rotation.

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:23.780
Keys move through lifecycle states such as created, active, expired, and revoked.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.440
Expired keys are not usually used for new protection, but they may still be needed to unprotect existing payloads.

6
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:38.700
That distinction matters for cookies, workflow links, temporary state, and any stored protected values that outlive a single request.

7
00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.380
Automatic key management reduces custom cryptographic burden.

8
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.160
Developers do not have to design low-level key rotation themselves for normal Data Protection use.

9
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.180
But automatic management does not remove deployment responsibility.

10
00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:02.960
The application still needs durable storage, environment separation, access control, backup planning, and an understanding of how key changes affect users.

11
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:12.760
Rotation should be understood before changing key storage, application name, deployment slots, hosting topology, or shared-cookie configuration.

12
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.820
A change that seems operational can affect whether existing payloads remain readable.

13
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.920
That can result in user sign-outs, invalid workflow links, or forms failing validation.

14
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.400
Revoking or deleting keys can make existing protected payloads unreadable.

15
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:36.860
Sometimes revocation is the right response to a key compromise, but it should be treated as an incident decision with known user impact.

16
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:44.160
Backup and restore plans should also consider whether protected payloads must survive deployment failures or disaster recovery.
